      },
      "geometry": {
        "method": "Five fixed positive quadrature samples represent a Gaussian dust depth distribution of sigma 250 pc around a regional mean on the warped plane. The mean solves the dust-weighted probability that a Gaussian 367 pc old-bar tracer lies behind it. Positive height is nearer Earth. A fixed 50 pc Gaussian smooths regional mean transitions. Every patch keeps its measured sky position and compact depth equal to its transverse width. No random clump displacement is used.",
        "assumed": "The red-clump median is treated as an effective mean foreground column for the old light. The vertical distribution and shared depths of neighbouring patches are priors, not cloud tomography. The five quadrature surfaces correlate the unmeasured far-side morphology with the observed foreground; they are a finite approximation, not five measured physical sheets. All cameras integrate the same finite 3D objects.",
        "limitations": "The 250 pc width is represented by five weighted depths. This preserves the source column and avoids normal-direction smoothing of measured narrow filaments, at the cost of unresolved depth-correlation ambiguity. Fraction saturation exposes disagreement or optical selection bias. The same geometry is used for all stellar populations, although red clump stars constrain old light best."
